			<div class="content" id="post-content-3891881">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So this is an alternate earth that's Not Quite Right, spatially or otherwise, and once you walk into it, you get placed into a mindless dreamlike timeline of an anomalous facsimile of 'average middle-class life'?</p>
<p>And the historical theme connection is&#8230; the way it looks at standard middle-class life? I'm super unclear on what this has to do with history.</p>
<p>I don't know about this. It's very high-effort, clearly, but I found the video really underwhelming and unnecessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All access to the unredacted transcripts of expeditions into SCP-4000 are to be protected by Bergman/Lynch incapacitation memetic videos. The Department of Memetics has analyzed memetic corruption within the logs for SCP-4000 and decided that the footage in the Bergman/Lynch memes should be sourced from horror films and uploaded to a private Youtube channel for maximum effectiveness.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why? There are no details given on the memetic effect at hand with this anomaly. It's not even <em>mentioned in the Description</em> that memetics are involved.</p>
<p>I didn't like that all of Command's unprofessional comments were just <em>This statement has been flagged for potential memetic corruption</em>'d away when there are no details on a memetic effect even being involved in the Description. Clearly the implication is supposed to be that the log is the first memetic hazard event(s) they encountered, but I feel like the Description is almost a different anomaly than what happens in the log because the log has <em>all of the details</em> and the Description has barely any.</p>
<p>And &quot;mouth-entities&quot; drives me bonkers. It's used <em>so</em> many times and it reads <em>so</em> awkwardly. I see no reason that it can't be SCP-4000-2. &quot;SCP-4000-2 instances inhabit SCP-4000.&quot; If the reason is 'memetic influence' then sure, cool, but we still don't know what that influence actually <em>is.</em> There's not even a paragraph.</p>
<p>That's my issue with this, I think. This article bothers me because it's not written like an SCP article, and there's not enough of a meaningful format screw to make up for that like there was with your entry last year. If I were criticizing the in-universe writer, I would say &quot;why didn't you take all of those key elements of the anomaly that are messily revealed in the exploration and put them in the Description? That's what this format is for.&quot;</p>
<p>Given others' responses, I'm almost wondering if I'm missing something major here.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Edit: I was going to downvote because of the memetics stuff, but since that's fixed, novote. I don't particularly like this for the other reasons given (and seconding rume and MMG above), but I can respect the value of the narrative in that log.</p>

							</div>

								
